    "slug": "canonical-and-duplicate-content",
    "title": "Canonical Tags and Duplicate Content: How to Get Them Right",
    "description": "Use one canonical link per page that points to its own absolute URL. Learn the checks canonical, canonical_self and canonical_absolute and fix them.",
    "datePublished": "2026-10-15",
    "dateModified": "2026-10-15",
    "html": "<h1>Canonical tags and duplicate content: how to get them right</h1>\n<p>The same page is often reachable at many addresses. With and without <code>www</code>, with tracking parameters, with a trailing slash, over http and https. To a crawler these can be separate pages with identical content. A canonical tag says which one counts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"which-checks-cover-canonical\">Which checks cover canonical?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Check id</th><th>What it tests</th><th>Passes when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>canonical</code></td><td>A canonical link is present</td><td>The tag exists</td></tr><tr><td><code>canonical_self</code></td><td>The canonical points to this page</td><td>The canonical resolves to the scanned URL</td></tr><tr><td><code>canonical_absolute</code></td><td>The canonical is an absolute URL</td><td>It starts with a scheme like <code>https:</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>og_url_matches_canonical</code></td><td><code>og:url</code> agrees with the canonical</td><td>Both name the same page</td></tr><tr><td><code>meta_robots_conflict</code></td><td>Robots directives do not contradict</td><td>No <code>index</code> with <code>noindex</code></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"what-does-the-tag-look-like\">What does the tag look like?</h2>\n<pre><code>&lt;link rel=&quot;canonical&quot; href=&quot;https://example.com/care&quot;&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Use the full URL, with the scheme and host. A relative value like <code>/care</code> is easy to break and fails <code>canonical_absolute</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"when-should-the-canonical-point-elsewhere\">When should the canonical point elsewhere?</h2>\n<p>When the page is a deliberate copy: a print version, a filtered list, or a tracking-parameter variant. Then <code>canonical_self</code> will fail, and that is correct. Decide on purpose. If a page is meant to rank by itself, it should point to itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-are-the-common-mistakes\">What are the common mistakes?</h2>\n<ol><li>Canonical pointing at the home page from every page.</li><li>A canonical that differs from the redirect target or from <code>og:url</code>.</li><li>Two canonical tags that disagree.</li><li>A canonical pointing at a URL that is blocked or marked <code>noindex</code>. See <a href=\"/learn/robots-txt-ai-crawlers\">robots.txt and AI crawlers</a>.</li><li>Mixing <code>index</code> and <code>noindex</code> in one robots meta.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"how-do-you-reduce-duplicate-content-in-the-first-place\">How do you reduce duplicate content in the first place?</h2>\n<ol><li>Redirect <code>http</code> to <code>https</code> and pick one hostname.</li><li>Pick a trailing slash policy and keep it.</li><li>Keep parameters out of internal links.</li><li>Give each page its own title and description. See <a href=\"/learn/meta-descriptions\">meta descriptions</a>.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"key-takeaways\">Key takeaways</h2>\n<ul><li>One absolute canonical per page, normally pointing at itself.</li><li>Point it elsewhere only for true copies.</li><li>Keep <code>og:url</code> equal to the canonical.</li><li>More SEO ids are in <a href=\"/learn/seo-checks\">every SEO check</a>.</li></ul>",
    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "What is a canonical tag?",
        "a": "It is a link in the head that names the preferred address for a page. Search engines use it to pick one URL when the same content is reachable at several."
      },
      {
        "q": "Should a page point its canonical at itself?",
        "a": "Usually yes. A self-referencing canonical is the safe default. Point elsewhere only when the page really is a copy of another."
      },
      {
        "q": "Is the canonical a command?",
        "a": "No. It is a strong hint. Search engines can pick a different URL if other signals disagree."
      },
      {
        "q": "Why does findable skip canonical_self?",
        "a": "It needs a canonical to compare against. With no canonical tag, canonical_self and canonical_absolute are skipped and canonical fails."
      }
    ]
